Digital | Basics of HDL. Consider the following RTL module (given in Verilog and VHDL):
module seq(input clk, input reset, input sel, input d, output reg q);
always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
  if (reset) q <= 1'b0;
  else if (sel) q <= ~q;
  else q <= d;
end
endmodule
(The VHDL version is equivalent: asynchronous reset to '0'; on rising_edge(clk), q_reg <= not q_reg if sel = '1', else q_reg <= d.)
Starting from the reset state, the following inputs are applied at four consecutive rising edges: Edge 1: sel=0, d=1; Edge 2: sel=1, d=0; Edge 3: sel=1, d=1; Edge 4: sel=0, d=0. What is the final value of q?
A. 0  B. 1  C. X  D. Z

Digital | Basics of C programming. What will be the output of the program?
#include <stdio.h>
#define $ a
#define _ b
static int $, _;
int main() {
  $ = _ = 2;
  printf("%d ", (fun(fun($, _), fun($, _))));
  return 0;
}
fun(int $, int _) {
  $ = $ >>= _ <<= 2;
  return _;
  return $;
}
A. 32  B. 0  C. 8  D. Compile time error

Digital | Basics of C programming. What will be the output of the program?
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
  int N = 1111101;
  int a = 2;
  int res = 0;
  while (N != 0) {
    res = res + (N % 10) * a;
    N = N / 10;
    a = a * 2;
  }
  printf("%d", res);
}
A. 250  B. 227  C. 251  D. Compile time error

Digital | Microprocessors and Microcontrollers. Consider the following pseudocode executed by a microcontroller:
count = 0
while (count < 5) {
  if (INPUT == HIGH) count = count + 2;
  else count = count + 1;
}
If INPUT remains HIGH throughout the execution, what will be the final value of count?
A. 4  B. 5  C. 6  D. 7

